## Source progress — 2026-11-10: bounded AWDL action-frame ingress

`src/wifi/AwdlAction.{hpp,cpp}` is compiled into the **ordinary** Xcode
production source target. It parses the documented 802.11 vendor-specific
AWDL action format (Apple BSSID and OUI, type 8, version 1.0, PSF/MIF),
reads both little-endian 32-bit transmit timestamps, and bounds each
16-bit-length TLV before publishing a header/count snapshot. It permits
only the documented one- or two-octet zero padding and rejects malformed
lengths, fragments, wrong vendor/version, unauthorized protected frames and
aliased output. Individual TLV payload views remain borrowed until the RX
owner releases the frame. Unknown TLV values are deliberately opaque.

Evidence: the independent AWDL layout described by Stute et al.,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.03156, and Project Zero's AWDL protocol
analysis:
https://projectzero.google/2020/12/an-ios-zero-click-radio-proximity.html.
`tests/test_awdl_action.cpp` exercises synthetic PSF/MIF, negative wire
syntax, aliasing and short/trailing data. Focused C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan,
strict Clang-Tidy on both new translation units, and formatting passed.
The latest full `tools/validate.py --tidy --tests --json` reported **582
checks, 548 passes, 34 previously known Tidy failures, zero skips** and no
new failing files. Existing strict Tidy violations remain unresolved.

**Not integrated yet:** physical RF RX provenance, native AWDL interface,
peer election/timing, channel dwell, regulatory scheduling, BLE service
policy, AirDrop/Continuity host linkage and actual IPv6 transfer. Merely
compiling this fail-closed syntax parser does not imply any macOS AWDL or
Bluetooth functionality. This P0 TODO remains open.

The same production source now also parses **read-only AWDL IPv6 data ingress**:
`parseAwdlIpv6Data` requires a complete, unprotected, nonfragmented
three-address 802.11 data/QoS packet carrying the Apple LLC/SNAP AWDL
protocol header, reserved-zero 16-bit field and IPv6 EtherType. It checks
IPv6 version and exact advertised payload length, then publishes copied
link metadata, inner sequence number and IPv6 byte offsets/lengths. The
same synthetic fixture covers valid normal/QoS frames and rejects wrong
vendor headers, To-DS routing, non-IPv6 data, fragment sequences,
truncation, wrong IP version and mismatched IPv6 length. This parser does
**not** produce an Ethernet frame, an IPv6 socket or a native AWDL interface.
The LLC/data header interpretation follows the same published AWDL research
and the 2026-11-04 brcmfmac AWDL data-path patch series:
https://lore-kernel.gnuweeb.org/brcm80211/20261104151547.70333-6-dev@deq.rocks/

## Source progress — 2026-11-10: bounded AWDL IPv6 TX framing

The ordinary Xcode target's `src/wifi/AwdlAction.{hpp,cpp}` also exports
`makeAwdlIpv6Data`. This serializer constructs an FCS-free, unprotected
three-address AWDL 802.11 Data or QoS Data MPDU, Apple AWDL BSSID and
LLC/SNAP header, inner AWDL sequence, reserved-zero field and IPv6 EtherType.
It accepts only a complete IPv6 packet with matching 16-bit payload length,
valid source/unicast or multicast destination, a bounded MAC sequence and an
MPDU no larger than 2346 octets including the radio-owned four-byte FCS.
No mutation occurs on failed bounds/alias checks; overlapping caller input,
output and count storage are rejected before a write. No key or cipher state
is inferred from a constructed packet.

`tests/test_awdl_action.cpp` round-trips normal/QoS serialization through
the independent RX parser, exercises source/destination, sequence and IPv6
length rejection, byte capacity and buffer aliasing, and covers the exact
maximum FCS-free MPDU boundaries. C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan host regressions,
strict Clang-Tidy on edited source and test units, and Clang-Format passed.
These are offline source tests only. No native AWDL host interface, peer
scheduler, real radio TX, encryption or AirDrop traffic has been connected;
peer/channel/keys/regulatory authority remain mandatory outside the
serializer. This P0 task remains open.

## Source progress — 2026-11-10: bounded PSF/MIF TX envelopes

`makeAwdlAction` now emits the documented vendor action-frame envelope for
PSF or MIF, with Apple AWDL BSSID/OUI, subtype/version, caller-supplied
transmit/target timestamps and bounded, structurally validated opaque TLVs.
It rejects malformed or missing TLVs, wrong subtype, invalid station/peer
addresses, a wrapped MAC sequence, oversized MPDUs and overlapping output,
input or length storage. A test compares its exact PSF frame bytes with an
independently constructed on-air fixture and round-trips a MIF through the
RX parser, including rejection cases. C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan, strict
Clang-Tidy and Clang-Format pass for the modified source/tests.

This only produces the outer MPDU framing. The required scheduler still has
to construct semantically correct election, availability and service TLVs,
and independently obtain native AWDL host, timing, peer, channel and RF TX
authorization. This does not grant actual peer discovery or AirDrop readiness.

## Source progress — 2026-11-10: bounded AWDL peer eligibility state

The normal production target's `AwdlAction.{hpp,cpp}` now provides a fixed
16-neighbor, allocation-free `AwdlNeighbors` table. An observed MIF must
carry exactly one well-formed nonzero Version (21), Synchronization (4),
Channel Sequence (18) and Election-v2 (24) TLV; duplicate or incomplete
required elements do not create a peer. Unknown TLVs remain opaque. The
parser copies source/master addresses, election metrics/counters, the
synchronization sequence and 16-slot channel-6 mask. It recognizes simple,
legacy and operating-class channel encodings, without mistaking advertised
5 GHz slots for AR9565-supported 2.4 GHz availability. A peer with no
advertised channel-6 slot has a zero mask and no inferred TX permission.

Only a valid MIF can create/update an entry. A syntactically valid IPv6
AWDL data MPDU may refresh an existing live neighbor but not create one.
Entries expire at the exact five-second lifetime boundary, when the
owner explicitly expires them, or on an epoch/reset `clear()`. Clock
rollback is refused without extending a session. Malformed input leaves
eligible peers untouched. The owner must pass FCS-verified RX and a
monotonic clock; there is no implicit driver/RF callback, election or TX.
The existing AWDL fixture now exercises copied TLV fields, no-social-slot
peers, duplicate/missing/invalid required TLVs, PSF rejection, 16-entry
capacity, time rollback, expiry and IPv6 refresh. Focused C++14/C++17
ASan+UBSan and direct strict Clang-Tidy passed.

This is a software neighbor-state building block, not operational AWDL:
no real native RX delivery, radio channel-tune/window scheduler, Apple
`awdl0` service, BLE-to-AWDL handoff, mDNS or AirDrop peer transfer is
connected yet. The P0 TODO remains open.

Source for the advertised peer eligibility and five-second timeout:
https://github.com/t4t5/owly/blob/main/docs/SPEC.md (AWDL-17).
This independent community protocol draft is not an Apple ABI contract.

The channel-sequence decoder was subsequently checked against the
independent AWDL-10 layout: simple and operating-class encodings put the
channel first, while legacy encoding puts the channel second. The decoded
16-slot channel-6 mask additionally requires operating class 81 for an
opclass entry, so a channel number inside an incompatible class cannot
misrepresent AR9565 2.4-GHz availability. Offline synthetic MIFs exercise
all three encodings, channel-6 acceptance and incompatible class rejection.
This prevents a peer advertisement from becoming an unverified RF grant.

## Source progress — 2026-11-10: guarded channel-6 window candidates

The normal production target's `planAwdlChannelSixRendezvous` evaluates a
bounded, **read-only candidate** for unicast AWDL channel-6 timing from a
verified MIF peer snapshot and an independently hardware-timestamped RX
sample. The 16 channel slots each span four 16-TU availability windows:
its sync down-counter is to the next **64-TU extended AW boundary**, not
an individual 16-TU boundary. The planner advances the 16-bit AW counter,
selects the corresponding advertised peer channel slot and enforces 3-TU
entry/exit guards plus declared packet airtime. It rejects absent channel-6
slots, invalid 1–64 TU down-counters, callback-clock rollback, stale MIF
phase at five seconds and timing samples not matching the most recent MIF.
`lastMifMs` is separate from neighbor lifetime: IPv6 data can keep a peer
present without forging a new timing anchor.

The independent schedule model was checked against the published OWL
`src/sync.c`, `src/schedule.c` and `src/rx.c` algorithms and AWDL-7/10/11/12
in https://github.com/t4t5/owly/blob/main/docs/SPEC.md. No OWL source was
copied. Synthetic tests cover the EAW boundary, first/last guard, exact
packet-fit limit, 16-bit sequence wrap, slot 0/10 vs unsupported slots,
clock and airtime rejection, MIF expiry and peer lifetime.
C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan, direct strict Clang-Tidy and formatting passed.

A candidate is **never** a radio TX authorization. The native owner still
must establish real RX TSF/monotonic calibration, master-election phase,
local radio channel-6 dwell, regulatory/transmit-power clearance,
coexistence with the associated Wi-Fi and Bluetooth sessions, native AWDL
interface ownership, peer security, DMA TX completion and an actual
AirDrop/Continuity host integration. No such runtime links were added here;
this P0 TODO remains open. Offline checks do not establish physical timing.

## Source progress — 2026-11-10: next guarded channel-six candidate

The production `AwdlAction` source now exposes
`planNextAwdlChannelSixRendezvous`, a fixed 17-EAW search for the next
*complete* unicast packet opportunity on an MIF-advertised channel-six
slot. It returns a bounded lead time and the channel/EAW sequence at the
first usable instant. Current and future opportunities exclude the first
and last three TU of each EAW, reject frames that do not fit, and never
extend past the five-second MIF synchronization lifetime. The immediate
candidate planner also rejects frames whose airtime reaches that expiry.
An unsigned-underflow condition during the final EAW guard is explicitly
avoided. Synthetic tests cover a current EAW, future channel transitions,
skipped off-channel slots, final-guard overflow, 16-bit sequence wrap,
no social slot, stale timing and invalid MIF anchors. Direct strict
Clang-Tidy and formatting pass; C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan host tests pass.

This remains read-only planning rather than radio authorization: local
channel tune, peer trust, regulatory clearance, coexistence, native AWDL
ownership, physical timestamp alignment and actual TX are unimplemented.
The P0 acceptance checklist remains open.

